¶
⟦200⟧represents the Slavonic languages fairly well, while Latin or Greek equivalents are given as the most familiar examples of I.E. Hypothetical forms are starred.

⟦300⟧{{smaller block|1. I.E. ''ī'' becomes (>) ''i'', ''gosti'': ''hostīs'' (acc. pl.); I.E. ''ĭ''>''ĭ'', ''vĭdova'': ''vidua''; I.E. ''j''>''j'', ''jucha'' : ''jus'' (broth).}}

⟦400⟧{{smaller block|2. I.E. ''ē'' becomes ''ě'', ''sěmę'': ''sēmen''; I.E. ''ĕ''> ''ĕ'' ''berą'': ''fero''.}}

⟦500⟧{{smaller block|3. I.E. ''ă'' and ''ŭ'' are alike ''o'' in Sl., ''orati'': ''arare''; ''osmĭ'': ''octo''; I.E. ''ŏ'' in end syllables, >''ŭ''; ''vozŭ'': ὄχος; I.E. ''ā'' and ''ō'' are alike ''a'', ''bratrŭ'': ''frater''; ''dŭva'': ''duo''.}}

⟦600⟧{{smaller block|4. I.E. ''ū'' becomes ''y'', ''ty'': ''tu''; I.E. ''ŭ''>''ŭ'', ''snŭcha'': ''nurus'', Sanskr. ''snušā'' : I.E. ''u̯''>''v'', ''vezą'': ''veho''.}}

⟦700⟧{{smaller block|5. I.E. ''r̥'' and ''l̥'' both long and short survived as vowels, *''vl̥kŭ'' written ''vlĭkŭ'', Sanskr. ''vr̥kas'', “ wolf ”; consonantal ''r'' and ''l'' survived unchanged.}}

⟦800⟧6. I.E. ''m̥'' and ''n̥'' both long and short: the former gave ''ĭ'' or ''ŭ''; ''sŭto'': ''centum''; the latter ''ę'' or ''ą'', ''desętĭ'': ''decem''. Consonantal ''m'' and ''n'' mostly survived before a vowel, after it they coalesced with it to make the nasal vowels ''ą'' and ''ę''; ''pąti'': ''pontis''; ''pętŭ'': πέμπτος.

⟦1000⟧{{smaller block|7. I.E. Aspirates are represented by corresponding sonants, ''berą'': ''fero''; ''medŭ'' (“ honey,” “ mead ”): μέθυ; ''mĭgla'': ὀμίχλη.}}

⟦1100⟧{{smaller block|8. I.E. ''s'' often becomes ''ch''; ''vetŭchŭ'': ''vetus''; not always, ''synŭ'': Lithu. ''sūnùs'', “ son ”; otherwise ''ch'' generally renders Gothic ''h'' in loan words; ''chlěbŭ'': ''hlaibs'', “ loaf ”; ''chyzŭ'': ''hus'', “ house.”}}

⟦1200⟧9. I.E. velar gutturals ''k'', ''g'', ''gh'' and labio-velars, ''q'', ''g'', ''gh'' become in Sl. ''k'', ''g'', ''g'', ''ključĭ'': ''clavis''; ''ąglŭ'': ''angulus''; ''mĭgla'': ὀμίχλη; ''kŭto'': ''quis'', ''govedo'': βοῦς, Sanskr. ''gāúš''; ''sněgŭ'': ''nix'', ''nivem'', but the Palato-gutturals ''k̑'', ''ĝ'', ''ĝh'' become Sl. ''s'', ''z'', ''z''; ''desętĭ'': ''decem''; ''zrĭno'': ''granum''; ''zima'': ''hiems''; Lithu. ''š'', ''ž'', ''ž''; ''deszimtis'', ''żirnis'', ''żěma''.

⟦1400⟧{{smaller block|10. (''a'') Gutturals ''k'', ''g'', ''ch'' (for ''s'') before ''e'', ''ě'' (for ''ē''), ''i'', ''ĭ'', ''ę'' and ''j'' early in the Proto-Sl. period became ''č'', ''ž'', ''š'', ''vlĭče'', voc. of ''vlĭkŭ'': λύκε; ''želądi'': ''glandis''; ''plušę'', 3rd pl. fr. ''pluchŭ'': ἔπλευσαν.}}

⟦1500⟧(''b'') Later ''k'', ''g'', ''ch'' before ''ě'', ''i'' (for ''oi'' or ''ai''), and sometimes after ''ĭ'', ''i'', ''ę'', > ''c'', ''dz'' (''z''), ''s''. ''Vlĭcě'' loc. cf. οἴκοι; ''lęzi'', imperat. of ''lęgą'', “ lie ”: λέγοις; ''dusi'', ''dusěchŭ'', nom. loc. pl. of ''duchŭ'', “ spirit ”; ''kŭnęzĭ'': Ger. ''kuning'': “ king.”

⟦1700⟧{{smaller block|(''c'') I.E. or Proto-Sl. ''sj'', ''zj'' became ''š'', ''ž'', ''šiti'', Lithu. ''siuti'', Lat. ''suo'', “ sew ”; ''nožĭ'' for *''nozjo'', “ knife.”}}

⟦1800⟧{{smaller block|(''d'') Non-guttural consonants followed by ''j'' (''tj'', ''dj'', ''nj''; ''pj'', ''bj'', ''vj'', ''mj'') gave different results (except ''nj'') in different languages (see below No. V.), but in Proto-Sl. there was already a tendency for the ''j'' to melt into and so change the consonant.}}

⟦1900⟧{{smaller block|11. Proto-Sl. gradually got rid of all its closed syllables, hence—}}

⟦2000⟧(''a'') Final consonants were dropped. ''Domŭ'': ''domus''.

⟦2000⟧{{smaller block|(''b'') Diphthongs became simple vowels ''ai'', ''oi'' > ''ě''; ''lěvŭ'': ''laevus''; ''vědě'': οἶδα; ''ei'' > ''i''; ''vidŭ'': εἶδος; ''au'', ''eu'', ''ou'' > ''u''; ''ucho'': ''auris''.}}

⟦2100⟧12. Proto-Slavonic had long, short and very short or half vowels (those expressed above by ''ĭ'' and ''ŭ''). It had a musical accent, free in its position with different intonations when it fell upon long syllables. (For the fate of these in different modern languages see below, No. VIII.)

⟦2300⟧13. The phenomena of vowel gradation (''Ablaut'') as presented by Slavonic are too complicated to be put shortly. In the main they answer to the I.E., ''e.g.'' O.S. ''bĭrati'', ''berą'', ''sŭborŭ'': δί-φρος, φέρω, φόρος.

⟦2500⟧In their morphology the Sl. languages have preserved or developed many interesting forms. Nouns have three genders, three numbers in O.S., Slovene, Serbo-Croat and Sorb (other tongues have more or less numerous traces of the Dual), and, except Bulgarian, seven cases—Nom., Voc. (not in Gt. Russian or Slovene), Acc., Gen., Dat., Instrumental and Locative. The Abl. has coincided with the Genitive.

⟦2700⟧The ''-o'', ''-a'' and ''-i'' declensions have gained at the expense of the consonantal stems, and phonetic change has caused many cases to coincide especially in the ''-i'' decl. The comparative of the Adj. is formed on I.E. models with ''š'' < ''sj'' corresponding to Latin ''r'' < ''s'', ''mĭnĭi'', gen. ''mĭnĭša'', cf. ''minus'', ''minoris''. The pronominal declension is less well preserved. There is no article, but ''i'' (ὅς) has been added to the adj. to make it definite; also in Bulgarian and in some dialects of Russian ''tŭ'' is postfixed as a real article.

⟦3100⟧{{smaller block|The Sl. verb has lost most of the I.E. voices, moods and tenses}}
